---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import { getLangFromUrl, useTranslatedPath } from '../../../i18n/utils';

const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url);
const translatePath = useTranslatedPath(lang);

const keywords =
  'online qibla, qibla direction, qibla direction arrow, live gibla direction online, compass qibla, find qibla direction, quick qibla direction, where is qibla direction, qibla direction compass degrees, qibla finder, qibla direction compass, online qibla direction, online qibla finder, online qibla compass, online qibla location';

const title = 'Qibla Direction in Compass Degrees: How the Bearing Is Calculated';
const description =
  'How the qibla direction compass degrees value is worked out: coordinates, the great-circle bearing, magnetic declination, and two worked examples.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} keywords={keywords} type="article">
  <Nav />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-[48rem] px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24">
    <a
      href={translatePath('/onlineqibla/blog/')}
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-body hover:text-ink transition-colors mb-8">← Back to Qibla Blog</a
    >
    <h1 class="font-sans text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mb-2">
      Qibla Direction in Compass Degrees: How the Bearing Is Calculated
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono mb-8">November 7, 2026</p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Two people stand on the same street, open the same <strong>online qibla</strong> page, and get numbers six degrees apart. Neither is broken. One is reading a bearing calculated from their coordinates, the other is reading a phone heading that has drifted. Knowing which number you are looking at is the whole skill.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      This guide takes you from a <strong>qibla direction</strong> you only half trust to a degrees value you can write down and follow. It covers where the number comes from, how the great-circle bearing is calculated, why a <strong>qibla direction compass degrees</strong> reading and your phone heading disagree, and two worked examples. It uses the <a href="/onlineqibla/" class="text-link hover:underline">Qibla finder</a> on this site, but the arithmetic is the same in any <strong>online qibla finder</strong>.
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Before you start: the three numbers that matter
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Every <strong>qibla direction compass</strong> reading is built from three numbers. Learn to separate them and the reading stops being a mystery.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>Your latitude and longitude</strong> — the coordinates the bearing is measured from.</li>
      <li><strong>The Kaaba coordinates</strong> — 21.4225° N, 39.8262° E, a fixed point.</li>
      <li><strong>Your heading</strong> — which way the device is actually pointing, in degrees clockwise from north.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The first two produce the <strong>qibla direction</strong>. The third is how you compare it with the room you are standing in. Mix the two up and a correct calculation looks wrong — and that mix-up is behind most attempts to find qibla direction that end in doubt.
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 1: Get an honest online qibla location
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The bearing is only as good as your coordinates. A phone using GPS is typically within 5–20 metres outdoors, which moves the <strong>qibla direction</strong> by a fraction of a degree — far too small to matter. Indoor location is the problem case: with no satellite fix the browser falls back to Wi-Fi and cell towers, and a city-centre estimate can be a kilometre or more out.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      For a reliable <strong>online qibla location</strong>, step outside or stand near a window for a few seconds before you trust the reading. If your <strong>live gibla direction online</strong> value jumps around by several degrees while you hold still, the coordinates are probably still settling.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li>Allow location permission once, then reload rather than re-prompting.</li>
      <li>Wait for the accuracy to settle before you read the number.</li>
      <li>In a basement or an interior room, step to a window for the fix, then walk back.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 2: Turn two coordinates into a bearing
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The bearing to the Kaaba is a great-circle initial bearing — the direction you would set off on if you walked the shortest path across the globe. It is calculated with the standard formula:
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      <code class="font-mono text-sm">θ = atan2( sin Δλ · cos φ₂ , cos φ₁ · sin φ₂ − sin φ₁ · cos φ₂ · cos Δλ )</code>
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      where φ₁, λ₁ are your coordinates, φ₂, λ₂ are the Kaaba’s, and Δλ is the longitude difference. The result is normalised to 0–360°. You do not need to do this by hand — the <strong>qibla finder</strong> does it as you move — but the shape of the formula explains two things people find odd.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li>The bearing is <strong>not</strong> a straight line on a flat map. On a Mercator map of Europe and the Middle East the true <strong>qibla direction</strong> curve looks wrong and the flat-map straight line looks right. The curve is right.</li>
      <li>The bearing changes with distance. From Jakarta the <strong>qibla direction</strong> is roughly 295°, from Cairo roughly 136°. Same target, opposite ends of the dial.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 3: Read the qibla direction arrow correctly
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      On screen you get two things: a number in degrees and a <strong>qibla direction arrow</strong>. The number is the bearing from true north. The arrow rotates with your device so it always points at the Kaaba relative to the phone, not relative to the room.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li>Hold the phone flat, screen up, away from steel and magnets.</li>
      <li>Turn your body — not just the phone — until the arrow points straight up the screen.</li>
      <li>Read the <strong>qibla direction compass degrees</strong> value as a cross-check: it should match the number printed on the dial.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      If you need a <strong>quick qibla direction</strong> check rather than a precise one, the arrow alone is enough. If you are marking a wall or laying out a prayer space, use the degrees value and a real compass, because a phone case with a magnetic clasp can shift a heading several degrees.
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Step 4: Allow for magnetic north
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A <strong>compass qibla</strong> reading taken from a magnetic needle points at magnetic north, not true north. The gap between them is magnetic declination, and it varies by place — a few degrees in most of Europe and the Middle East, much more at high latitudes. A browser reading a device sensor usually reports a heading that already accounts for it; a physical needle does not.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">City</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Approx. declination</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">What it does to a needle reading</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Patna, IN</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">about 0.5° E</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Negligible</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">London, UK</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">about 0.5° E</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Negligible</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">New York, US</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">about 13° W</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Needle reading must be corrected east</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Cape Town, ZA</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">about 25° W</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Large enough to reverse a direction check</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Reykjavík, IS</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">about 12° W</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Correct before marking a wall</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Values drift by a fraction of a degree a year and shift across a country, so treat the table as an order of magnitude, not a lookup.
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Worked example 1: a flat in Patna
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A user in Patna (25.59° N, 85.14° E) opens the page indoors, gets a Wi-Fi estimate, then steps onto a balcony. Two readings:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Reading</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Value</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">What it means</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Indoor Wi-Fi fix</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">272°</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Bearing is fine, coordinates were off by about 800 m</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Balcony GPS fix</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">274°</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Same calculation, better input</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Phone heading</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">281°</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Sensor drift from a metal balcony rail</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The bearing was never the problem. The <strong>qibla direction</strong> from Patna is around 274° — a little south of due west — and both fixes land within two degrees once the coordinates are honest. The 281° heading came from the device, not the maths.
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Worked example 2: a hotel room in Istanbul
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Istanbul (41.01° N, 28.98° E) gives a <strong>qibla direction</strong> of roughly 152°, so the arrow points south-south-east. Standing in an unfamiliar room there is no marker to check against, which is why the <em>where is qibla direction</em> search is so common on the first night of a trip.
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
      <li>Open the <strong>online qibla compass</strong>, allow location, and read the degrees value — 152°.</li>
      <li>Screenshot it. The bearing does not change while you stay in the city.</li>
      <li>Find the hotel room’s orientation from the window and corridor, then rotate the phone until the arrow matches.</li>
      <li>Mark the direction with a folded towel or a luggage tag so the next prayer is a glance, not a recalculation.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      Troubleshooting: when the number will not settle
    </h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-body border border-hairline">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Problem</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Likely cause</th>
            <th class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold text-ink">Fix</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Arrow spins while the phone is still</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Compass sensor uncalibrated</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Figure-eight the phone twice, then re-open the page</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Reading differs from a friend’s phone</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">One device is in a case with a magnet</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Remove the case and compare again</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Degrees value jumps by tens</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">GPS still settling, or indoor Wi-Fi location</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Move to a window or outdoors and wait for the fix</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Bearing looks 15–20° off from a paper map straight line</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Flat-map line instead of great circle</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Trust the calculated bearing, not the ruler</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Arrow points at the wrong wall in a new building</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Steel frame or lift shaft nearby</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Step two metres away and recheck</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Page shows no direction at all</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Location permission blocked</td>
            <td class="border border-hairline px-3 py-2">Allow location for the site, then reload</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h2 class="font-sans text-xl sm:text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink mt-10 mb-3">
      The short version
    </h2>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The <strong>qibla direction</strong> is a calculated bearing, not a sensor reading: your coordinates plus the Kaaba’s, run through the great-circle formula. The <strong>qibla direction arrow</strong> is that bearing drawn onto your device heading, which is why it is the part that wobbles. Read the degrees value first, let the location fix settle, and correct for declination if you are comparing with a physical needle. Everything else is calibration.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Open the <a href="/onlineqibla/" class="text-link hover:underline">online qibla finder</a> and check the number against the table above — if the degrees value is steady and the location is accurate, the <strong>online qibla direction</strong> you are reading is right to within a couple of degrees.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
